ound, and micro-information at the bottom enter atomized out-of-focus. The text uses modern sans-serif fonts, white or near-white, with short, heavy, bold titles that look like they are printed on the glass and slightly bitten by the surface layer. Corner identifiers, edge dates, numbers, and micro-annotations at the bottom form a metadata coordinate system like work numbers, archive records, and production parameters, serving the reading path without covering the clear narrow window. The entire surface retains visible tactile embossed glass texture, with texture density changing with the brightness of the underlying layer—denser in dark parts, milkier in bright parts, with fine refraction and slight displacement at the edges. All images, objects, and text look unified under the same material. The final image should present a calm, restrained visual cover with a technical archival feel: information is intentionally hidden, and only the most critical parts are released.

